#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""
audacity-quicksave
==================

A real "Save" for Audacity on Linux: writes the audio you are editing back
over the original file, keeping its format, codec, bitrate, sample rate,
channels, tags and cover art. A backup of the previous version is kept.

How it works
------------
Audacity's mod-script-pipe module lets external programs send commands to
Audacity through two named pipes. When the keyboard shortcut runs this script,
it connects to Audacity and:

  1. finds the file the active project was imported from (Audacity's own
     "Recent Files" list, matched against the track names);
  2. exports the whole project to a temporary lossless 32-bit float WAV;
  3. re-encodes it with ffmpeg using the original file's characteristics;
  4. backs up the original and atomically replaces it;
  5. disconnects, so Audacity can still quit normally.

Requirements: Audacity 3.x with mod-script-pipe enabled, Python 3.8+, ffmpeg.
License: MIT.
"""

